{"kind":"assert","text":"Planner decision on 52ea1252. The read does not end at revocation. A retired or compromised key keeps its signed read for the rest of the window, and the scope does not ask membership at read time.\n\nWhy:\n- A read session already works this way. `packages/scope/src/sessions.ts` says a reading scope checks nothing of the key or the member after a revocation, and an issued session stays good for up to 600 seconds. Ending the signed read alone would leave the same key reading through its session.\n- The read is bounded. A revoked key's later acts are refused, so it cannot renew the window for long.\n- Asking membership adds a call between scopes to every signed read, plus a new failure when membership cannot answer. The founder's first reads, which are the reason for these reads, then depend on that call.\n\nThis weighs what 61cc5e50 did not: the summary holds a scope's live items, so a stolen key can read current items for the rest of the window. I accept that as a limit and require it stated.\n\nDo this under 09083039:\n1. Find the real bound from the code. Include any time in which a scope still accepts a revoked key's act, because each accepted act restarts the window. Hold the result in the test.\n2. State the limit in `docs/security/` with the session's: what a revoked key can still read, and for how long.\n3. State there that replacing the session secret ends sessions at once and does not end signed reads. An operator who responds to a stolen key needs that fact.\n\nIf step 1 shows that a revoked key can keep renewing the window, this decision does not hold. Stop and tell me. Reopen it too if a design makes a scope hold key standing at read time for another reason.","body":{"no_git_artifact":"true","to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}